umber.\\n   - **Return Result**:\\n     - Return the result of the prime check.\\n\\n3. **Boundary Conditions**:\\n   - Handle the case where the string is empty.\\n   - Handle the case where the string length is 1.\\n\\n4. **Edge Cases**:\\n   - Empty string: Return `False`.\\n   - Single-character string: Return `True`.\\n\\n5. **Complexity**:\\n   - The `is_prime` function has a time complexity of O(\\\\(\\\\sqrt{n}\\\\)).\\n   - The overall function has a time complexity of O(\\\\(\\\\sqrt{n}\\\\)) due to the length calculation and prime check.\\n\\n6. **Example Implementations**:\\n   ```python\\n   def is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\\n       if n <= 1:\\n           return False\\n       if n in [2, 3]:\\n           return True\\n       if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\\n           return False\\n       i = 5\\n       while i * i <= n:\\n           if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\\n               return False\\n           i += 6\\n       return True\\n\\n   def prime_length(string: str) -> bool:\\n       if not string:\\n           return False\\n       length = len(string)\\n       if length == 1:\\n           return True\\n       return is_prime(length)\\n   ```\\n\\nFollow this checklist to implement the `prime_length` function correctly."}'}]
